Determine the specific details of the mythological story involving Achilles' death, including the location and circumstances of the event
Achilles' death is a pivotal event in Greek mythology. According to legend, Achilles was killed by an arrow to his heel, which was his only vulnerable spot. The location of his death was the Trojan plain, near the city of Troy, during the Trojan War.

The circumstances of his death were as follows: Achilles was killed by an arrow shot by Paris, a prince of Troy, who was aided by the god Apollo. The arrow was shot into Achilles' heel, which was unprotected by his armor. This was because his mother, Thetis, had dipped him in the River Styx when he was a baby to make him invulnerable, but she held him by the heel, which was not touched by the water and therefore remained vulnerable.

Aphrodite, the goddess of love, intervened on behalf of Paris and helped him shoot the arrow that killed Achilles. The event marked a turning point in the Trojan War, as Achilles' death was a significant blow to the Greek army.
