What are the historical origins and significance of Rogation Days in the Christian church?
Rogation Days have their historical origins in the pagan Roman procession known as Robigalia, which was held to propitiate the deity of agricultural disease, Robigus, and to ask for protection of crops from wheat rust. The Christian major rogation replaced this pagan ritual and was introduced by Mamertus, bishop of Vienne, around AD 470. The minor rogation days were also introduced by Mamertus and were later adopted into the Roman rite. The significance of Rogation Days lies in their purpose of beseeching God for the appeasement of his anger, protection from calamities, and blessings for crops and farmers. The observance of Rogation Days involves prayer, fasting, and abstinence, as well as solemn processions and the recitation of the Litany of the Saints.
