What is regicide and its historical context?
Regicide is the deliberate killing of a monarch or the person responsible for the killing of a person of royalty. Historically, it refers to the judicial execution of a king after a trial, as seen in the case of Charles I of England. The term can also be applied to the killing of an emperor or any other reigning sovereign. Regicide has particular significance in the context of the divine right of kings, where monarchs were believed to have a divinely anointed authority to rule.
