What were the historical origins and development of The Flying Scotsman train service?
The Flying Scotsman train service originated in 1862 as the "Special Scotch Express" with simultaneous departures at 10:00 from London King's Cross and Edinburgh Waverley. The service was established by the East Coast Joint Stock, a joint agreement between the Great Northern Railway (GNR), the North British Railway (NBR), and the North Eastern Railway (NER). The original journey took hours, but with improvements in railway technology and increasing competition, the time was reduced to hours by the 1888 "Race to the North". The service was modernized in 1896, introducing new locomotives and corridor tenders, and by 1928, a non-stop service was introduced with special corridor tenders. The name "Flying Scotsman" was officially adopted in 1924, and the service has been operated by various railway companies, including London North Eastern Railway, since then.
