What was the significance of Heike Kamerlingh Onnes' research on the resistance of solid mercury at cryogenic temperatures in 1911?
Heike Kamerlingh Onnes' research on the resistance of solid mercury at cryogenic temperatures in 1911 led to the discovery of superconductivity, where he observed that the resistance in a solid mercury wire immersed in liquid helium suddenly vanished at a temperature of 4.2 K. This was a significant discovery, as it showed that certain materials can exhibit zero electrical resistance below a certain temperature.
