What is the structure of the British parliament?
The structure of the British parliament is as follows:

The British parliament is a bicameral legislature, consisting of two houses:

1.  The House of Commons: This is the lower house, composed of 650 elected Members of Parliament (MPs) who represent constituencies across the United Kingdom.
2.  The House of Lords: This is the upper house, composed of appointed members who hold their seats for life. The House of Lords has a smaller number of members, with around 790 peers.

The House of Commons is responsible for debating and voting on bills, while the House of Lords reviews and revises them. The monarch (currently the King or Queen) serves as the head of the parliament, but does not have the power to vote or make laws.
