What is the historical significance of Mons Meg and where is it located?
Mons Meg is a medieval bombard with historical significance as a powerful artillery piece used in sieges until the mid-16th century. It was built in 1449 and sent as a gift to James II, King of Scots in 1454. The gun was employed in several battles, including the siege of Norham Castle in 1513. Today, Mons Meg is located at Edinburgh Castle in Scotland, on loan to Historic Scotland from the Royal Armouries.
