What is the smallest unit of time in musical notation and what notes have this duration?
The smallest unit of time in musical notation is the "semibreve" or "whole note", but in modern notation, it is often referred to as the "whole note" or simply "whole". However, the smallest unit of time is actually the "semiquaver" or "sixteenth note". 

The notes that have a duration of a semiquaver (sixteenth note) are: 

- Sixteenth note (semiquaver)
- Sixty-fourth note (fourth of a sixteenth note)
- One hundred twenty-eighth note (half of a sixty-fourth note)
- Two hundred fifty-sixth note (half of a one hundred twenty-eighth note)
- Five hundred twelfth note (half of a two hundred fifty-sixth note)
