Explain the historical context and purpose of telephone lines in the early 20th century.
In the early 20th century, telephone lines were primarily used to connect rural areas to the public switched telephone network, where a single line would span miles from the nearest town to one property and on to the next. This was a cost-effective solution for corporations to provide telephone service to isolated communities. The lines were often shared among multiple subscribers, known as party lines, where several dozen telephones would be connected to a single line, allowing users to listen in on each other's conversations. The purpose of telephone lines in this era was to eliminate the need for human switchboard operators and provide a means for people to communicate over long distances, while also being a more affordable option for rural areas.
