What is the historical context of the Wild West in the United States?
The historical context of the Wild West in the United States refers to the period of time following the end of the American Civil War in 1869, characterized by the expansion of the United States westward, the settlement of the frontier, and the interaction between Native American tribes and American settlers. This era saw the rise of lawlessness, outlaws, and gunfights, as well as the growth of cities and the development of the railroad. The Wild West was also marked by the forced assimilation of Native American tribes into white culture, as seen in the establishment of Indian boarding schools like the Carlisle Indian Industrial School.

In the context of the provided texts, the Wild West is mentioned in relation to the film "Wild Wild West" (1999), which is set in this time period, and the concept of "Wild Westing", which refers to the portrayal of Native American tribes in dime novels, Wild West shows, and other forms of media.
