What is the definition of Michaelmas and its historical significance?
Michaelmas is a Christian festival observed on September 29, honoring the Archangel Michael and all angels. It has historical significance as one of the four quarter days of the financial year, marking the end of the harvest season and the beginning of a new cycle. In medieval England, it was a Holy Day of Obligation, but this tradition was abolished in the 18th century. The festival is also associated with the election of the new Lord Mayor of London and has been celebrated in various ways throughout history, including in art, literature, and culture.
