What were the 93rd Highlanders' actions and performance during the Battle of Balaclava, and how did their actions contribute to the overall outcome of the battle?
The 93rd Highlanders, under the command of Sir Colin Campbell, played a crucial role in the Battle of Balaclava on 25 October 1854. They formed the "Thin Red Line" and, despite being vastly outnumbered, successfully repelled a Russian cavalry charge. With around 200 men, aided by a small force of walking wounded and Turkish infantrymen, they held steady and broke the Russian cavalry charge using musket fire alone, without forming into a square. This action was witnessed by William Howard Russell, a correspondent for "The Times," who described the scene as a "thin red streak tipped with a line of steel." The 93rd Highlanders' bravery and discipline contributed significantly to the overall outcome of the battle, as they helped to secure the British base at Balaklava and ultimately forced the Russians onto the defensive.
